.8<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">3R2Q</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x02212;8.5<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;0.9<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">3R3Q<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-152">&#x02021;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;10.6<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;0.6</td></tr><tr><td align="left">3R4Q</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x02212;3.2<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">0.0</td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R1Q<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-152">&#x02021;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x0002b;4.7<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;2.0<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R2Q</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x02212;0.6</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;0.5<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R3Q<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-152">&#x02021;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;57.5<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x02212;2.6<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFIV-155">&#x02016;</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">4R4Q</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x0002b;0.2</td><td align="left"/><td align="left">&#x0002b;0.3</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="TFIV-150"><label>&#x0002a;</label><p>v<sub>1/2</sub> and a<sub>h</sub> are the half-maximal voltage and slope factor for fast inactivation. Data from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B21">St&#x000fc;hmer et al. (1989)</xref> did not include an assessment of statistical significance. &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-152"><label>&#x02021;</label><p>Data from Chen et al. (1966). &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-153"><label>&#x000a7;</label><p>Data from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B21">St&#x000fc;hmer et  al. (1989)</xref>. These data were acquired using a 100-ms prepulse, whereas all  other data were acquired using a 500-ms prepulse. &#x000a0;</p></fn><fn id="TFIV-155"><label>&#x02016;</label><p>Values statistically significant, with a probability &#x0003c;0.05 resulting from random variation.    &#x000a0;</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="TV"><label>Table V</label><caption><p>Comparison of Mutation Effects on Activation and Inactivation</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Mutant</th><th/><th>Activation v<sub>1/2</sub> shift<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-150">&#x0002a;</xref></th><th/><th>Fast inactivation v<sub>1/2</sub> shift<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-152">&#x02021;</xref></th><th/><th>Slow inactivation (no fast) v<sub>1/2</sub> shift<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-153">&#x000a7;</xref></th><th/><th>Slow inactivation (fast intact) v<sub>1/2</sub> shift<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-155">&#x02016;</xref></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td/><td/><td><italic>mV</italic></td><td/><td><italic>mV</italic></td><td/><td><italic>mV</italic></td><td/><td><italic>mV</italic></td></tr><tr><td align="left">1R2Q</td><td/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x02212;1.0</td><td/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x02212;5.1<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-176">176</xref></td><td/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x0002b;1.6</td><td/><td align="left">&#x02212;11.3<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-176">176</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left">1R2K</td><td/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x0002b;2.8</td><td/><td align="left">&#x000a0;&#x02212;9.9<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="TFV-176">176</xref></td><td/><td align="